WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.234 --> 00:00:03.269 line:0%
-There are more than
10 million species

00:00:03.269 --> 00:00:05.505 line:0%
of plant and animals
alive today.

00:00:05.505 --> 00:00:12.945 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:12.945 --> 00:00:15.948 line:0%
And, yet, they are just 1%

00:00:15.948 --> 00:00:18.518 line:0%
of all species
that have ever existed.

00:00:18.518 --> 00:00:23.622 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:23.622 --> 00:00:26.559 line:0%
By traveling back in time,

00:00:26.559 --> 00:00:30.029 line:0%
we'll meet many of the 99%
that have gone extinct.

00:00:30.029 --> 00:00:32.198 line:0%
[ Animal growls softly ]

00:00:32.198 --> 00:00:33.366
-Welcome back to "Late Night."

00:00:33.366 --> 00:00:35.501
We're here with Morgan Freeman,
everybody!

00:00:35.501 --> 00:00:36.736
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:36.736 --> 00:00:41.340
That was a clip
from "Life on Our Planet."

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:42.575
This shows us --
-Great show.

00:00:42.575 --> 00:00:44.344
It's a wonderful show.
It makes you appreciate

00:00:44.344 --> 00:00:46.746
being alive when you see
how this world of ours

00:00:46.746 --> 00:00:48.548
has developed
over billions of years.

00:00:48.548 --> 00:00:50.483
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:00:50.483 --> 00:00:53.519
And if we're not careful...
-It does.

00:00:53.519 --> 00:00:55.221
You know, it sort of
is a nice thing to watch

00:00:55.221 --> 00:00:57.156
where you realize
that climate change

00:00:57.156 --> 00:00:58.491
could take this all
away from us.

00:00:58.491 --> 00:00:59.993
Is that something
when you get involved

00:00:59.993 --> 00:01:01.894
with a project like this --
do you enjoy that

00:01:01.894 --> 00:01:03.596
it's not just
the wonder of the world...

00:01:03.596 --> 00:01:05.765
-Oh. Absolutely.
Absolutely, Seth.

00:01:05.765 --> 00:01:07.667
Um...

00:01:07.667 --> 00:01:12.005
We're in such a...

00:01:12.005 --> 00:01:13.906
flux here

00:01:13.906 --> 00:01:19.479
in terms of our existence
on this planet.

00:01:19.479 --> 00:01:25.818
Let's not think that
we're here to stay just because.

00:01:25.818 --> 00:01:27.654
We can see.

00:01:27.654 --> 00:01:33.125
If 99% of all life
that has ever lived is gone...

00:01:33.125 --> 00:01:35.494
and only 1% is left...

00:01:35.494 --> 00:01:37.597
we ought to think about that.

00:01:37.597 --> 00:01:40.934
It's like...
well, this planet does things.

00:01:40.934 --> 00:01:42.501
[ Laughter ]

00:01:42.501 --> 00:01:45.104
It's like --
And there's no place to go.

00:01:45.104 --> 00:01:47.006
-That's true.
-Absolutely no place to go.

00:01:47.006 --> 00:01:49.109
So we better be careful!

00:01:49.109 --> 00:01:51.544
-That's a good --
That's a good warning.

00:01:51.544 --> 00:01:54.881
You know, in the clip,
it showed, you know,

00:01:54.881 --> 00:01:56.349
dinosaurs, prehistoric life,

00:01:56.349 --> 00:01:58.618
and you have to do
a narration about that.

00:01:58.618 --> 00:02:00.386
And I'm wondering --
I thought I knew

00:02:00.386 --> 00:02:01.954
how to say
all the dinosaurs' names,

00:02:01.954 --> 00:02:03.923
but then I have kids, and they
have their dinosaur books,

00:02:03.923 --> 00:02:06.425
and I realize I only know
sort of the top three.

00:02:06.425 --> 00:02:07.527
-Yeah, me too.

00:02:07.527 --> 00:02:09.429
-And then they get
pretty tricky.

00:02:09.429 --> 00:02:10.964
Is that something --
Do you get a --

00:02:10.964 --> 00:02:12.832
I mean, I'm assuming
you get a bunch of takes,

00:02:12.832 --> 00:02:16.068
but are you good with those
words you've never seen before?

00:02:16.068 --> 00:02:17.470
-Let me not brag about

00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:19.505
what I shouldn't be
trying to brag about.

00:02:19.505 --> 00:02:22.708
-You are --
You are a humble man.

00:02:22.708 --> 00:02:25.611
And you are also --
We were talking backstage.

00:02:25.611 --> 00:02:29.516
You live in the country now.
You live on the Gulf Coast.

00:02:29.516 --> 00:02:33.586
-Yes.
Well, it's not "the country."

00:02:33.586 --> 00:02:37.023
-Oh.
I didn't mean it to be rude.

00:02:37.023 --> 00:02:39.258
-Well --
-You have stores and stuff?

00:02:39.258 --> 00:02:42.395
[ Laughter ]

00:02:42.395 --> 00:02:43.763
-Restaurants.

00:02:43.763 --> 00:02:44.964
-Restaurants?!
-Yeah.

00:02:44.964 --> 00:02:46.565
-Okay. I was thinking
of a different thing.

00:02:46.565 --> 00:02:48.501
-Yeah. Right.

00:02:48.501 --> 00:02:50.836
But I have a home
in Mississippi also.

00:02:50.836 --> 00:02:52.238
-Gotcha.

00:02:52.238 --> 00:02:55.007
-Pretty much where --
I grew up in Mississippi.

00:02:55.007 --> 00:02:57.843
Raise there.
And so I live there.

00:02:57.843 --> 00:03:00.913
But it's just a farm,
and it's --

00:03:00.913 --> 00:03:03.182
That's the country.
-That's the country.

00:03:03.182 --> 00:03:04.450
When did it dawn on you?

00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:06.419
Did you think
through your whole life

00:03:06.419 --> 00:03:08.954
that at one point you would
find your way back there?

00:03:08.954 --> 00:03:10.890
Or was it as you --
as you got older, you thought,

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:12.558
"Oh, you know what,
I like that."

00:03:12.558 --> 00:03:18.464
-No, it was not until
about 1983.

00:03:18.464 --> 00:03:21.401
My parents had moved back.

00:03:21.401 --> 00:03:25.004
And I started visiting them

00:03:25.004 --> 00:03:28.073
maybe once a year or so.

00:03:28.073 --> 00:03:32.278
And every time I went there,
I was like, "Oh.

00:03:32.278 --> 00:03:34.514
This is so relaxing.
This is so nice.

00:03:34.514 --> 00:03:35.881
This is...'

00:03:35.881 --> 00:03:40.886
So by 1989 or '90...

00:03:40.886 --> 00:03:43.589
we decided, alright,
we'll build here.

00:03:43.589 --> 00:03:46.259
-Are people happy to see you
back home in Mississippi?

00:03:46.259 --> 00:03:48.694
-Always. Yeah.
-Yeah, I would hope so.

00:03:48.694 --> 00:03:50.263
At, like, the stores
and the restaurants?

00:03:50.263 --> 00:03:52.564
-See that?
-What is that? S-I-P?

00:03:52.564 --> 00:03:54.500
-That's The Sip.
-What's The Sip?

00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:56.635
-That's --
-Oh, that's Mississippi!

00:03:56.635 --> 00:03:58.237
-Yeah! Yo!

00:03:58.237 --> 00:04:01.007
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:04:01.007 --> 00:04:03.676
-Now...

00:04:03.676 --> 00:04:05.845
I just want you to know I would
never have the confidence

00:04:05.845 --> 00:04:07.914
to say to you,
"How's The Sip? Is it good?

00:04:07.914 --> 00:04:10.249
Are things in The Sip good?"
But now I'm gonna --

00:04:10.249 --> 00:04:11.751
Next time you come here,
I'm gonna say it.

00:04:11.751 --> 00:04:13.953
-Okay. We're moving right along.

00:04:13.953 --> 00:04:15.721
-Alright. I'm glad.
-Yeah.

00:04:15.721 --> 00:04:18.124
Moving all the way
into the 20th century.

00:04:18.124 --> 00:04:20.326
-There we go.
-Yeah.

00:04:20.326 --> 00:04:22.695
It's certainly easier to spell.
Um...

00:04:22.695 --> 00:04:25.731
I want to ask about another
documentary you produced

00:04:25.731 --> 00:04:27.600
about --

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:29.102
It was -- What was it called?
"The 761st..."

00:04:29.102 --> 00:04:30.870
-It was -- Yeah.

00:04:30.870 --> 00:04:34.106
The 761st Tank Battalion.
The Black Panthers.

00:04:34.106 --> 00:04:40.813
This was the first
armored battalion, Black,

00:04:40.813 --> 00:04:46.319
to be engaged in the war --
World War II.

00:04:46.319 --> 00:04:48.687
Um...Patton.

00:04:48.687 --> 00:04:51.557
-And did you know much
about them?

00:04:51.557 --> 00:04:53.326
I know you served
in the Air Force.

00:04:53.326 --> 00:04:55.360
Was this something
you'd ever heard about?

00:04:55.360 --> 00:04:58.731
-No. I had -- A guy came to us.

00:04:58.731 --> 00:05:01.534
I have a company.

00:05:01.534 --> 00:05:03.836
You know, we make movies.
-You produce stuff.

00:05:03.836 --> 00:05:07.539
-Produce stuff, you know?
Revelations Entertainment.

00:05:07.539 --> 00:05:09.809
And the guy came to us
with this project.

00:05:09.809 --> 00:05:14.914
It was a script about
the 761st Tank Battalion.

00:05:14.914 --> 00:05:17.450
I did not know anything
about that.

00:05:17.450 --> 00:05:20.552
So, of course, you can Google it

00:05:20.552 --> 00:05:25.324
and find out everything
you need to know now.

00:05:25.324 --> 00:05:28.661
Um, so then -- I don't know.

00:05:28.661 --> 00:05:33.565
But this goes back
to 20, 25 years maybe...

00:05:33.565 --> 00:05:36.069
-Okay.
-...that we've had this project.

00:05:36.069 --> 00:05:40.539
So it's like...
nothing good comes easy.

00:05:40.539 --> 00:05:43.776
And then -- I don't know.

00:05:43.776 --> 00:05:47.013
As things developed,
we were able to make

00:05:47.013 --> 00:05:51.049
a very, very nice documentary
about them.

00:05:51.049 --> 00:05:54.353
But I want to make a series
to get them out into...

00:05:54.353 --> 00:05:57.056
-Yeah. Sort of a "Band of
Brothers" situation, right?

00:05:57.056 --> 00:05:58.691
-Exactly.
Exactly what I want to do.

00:05:58.691 --> 00:06:00.526
-I think that's a very
safe pair of hands.

00:06:00.526 --> 00:06:02.361
I hope you can get that
for us all to see.

00:06:02.361 --> 00:06:04.230
There's a wonderful book
about it, as well,

00:06:04.230 --> 00:06:05.965
called "Come Out Fighting."

00:06:05.965 --> 00:06:07.900 line:0%
I'm assuming
this is one of these...

00:06:07.900 --> 00:06:09.235 line:0%
-Yeah, we used that.

00:06:09.235 --> 00:06:11.737 line:0%
That's part of our background,
our research.

00:06:11.737 --> 00:06:13.171
-There you go.
-Yeah.

00:06:13.171 --> 00:06:15.474
-Alright. Well, it's just
a joy to have you back.

00:06:15.474 --> 00:06:16.743
Thank you so much.
What a pleasure.

00:06:16.743 --> 00:06:18.177
-It's a joy to sit here
and talk to you.

00:06:18.177 --> 00:06:19.946
-Morgan Freeman, everybody.

00:06:19.946 --> 00:06:22.581
"Life on Our Planet"
is streaming now on Netflix.

00:06:22.581 --> 00:06:24.717
We'll be right back
with McKay Coppins.

